Relevant columns and their potential usage  
• “country_code”: filter only Canadian (“CA”) publications  
• “publication_date”: derive the calendar year in order to build 5‑year buckets (e.g. 1960‑1964, 1965‑1969 … 2015‑2020)  
• “publication_number”: unique id of a patent publication – used to count how many Canadian publications fall in each 5‑year span  
• “inventor”: VARIANT array that lists every inventor – we flatten this array to count how many inventors a publication has  
• “family_id” (optional): lets you collapse all Canadian publications that belong to the same simple patent family, if you want the analysis at a family level instead of publication level  

Typical conditions you will need  
• WHERE “country_code” = 'CA'                                     -- keep only Canadian patents  
• WHERE “publication_date” BETWEEN 19600101 AND 20201231          -- keep publication years 1960‑2020  
• HAVING COUNT(DISTINCT inventor_name) > 0                        -- ignore records with no inventor  
• GROUP BY 5‑year bucket                                          -- create the desired time intervals  

Below are ten step‑by‑step Snowflake SQL queries (LIMIT 20 rows each) that move from quick inspections to the complete calculation.  
Every column name is enclosed in double quotes and we only use tables listed in the provided catalogue.

```sql
-- 1. Description: Peek at the distinct country codes that actually appear in the patent publication table
SELECT DISTINCT "country_code"
FROM PATENTS.PATENTS.PUBLICATIONS
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 2. Description: Count how many Canadian (“CA”) publications exist overall
SELECT COUNT(*) AS "ca_publication_cnt"
FROM PATENTS.PATENTS.PUBLICATIONS
WHERE "country_code" = 'CA'
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 3. Description: Look at a sample of Canadian publications with their raw inventor JSON to understand its structure
SELECT "publication_number",
       "publication_date",
       "inventor"
FROM PATENTS.PATENTS.PUBLICATIONS
WHERE "country_code" = 'CA'
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 4. Description: Flatten the inventor array for Canadian patents to list one row per inventor / publication
SELECT p."publication_number",
       f.value::STRING AS "inventor_name"
FROM PATENTS.PATENTS.PUBLICATIONS p,
     LATERAL FLATTEN(input => p."inventor") f
WHERE p."country_code" = 'CA'
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 5. Description: For each Canadian publication, compute the number of inventors attached to it
SELECT p."publication_number",
       COUNT(f.value) AS "inventor_cnt"
FROM PATENTS.PATENTS.PUBLICATIONS p,
     LATERAL FLATTEN(input => p."inventor") f
WHERE p."country_code" = 'CA'
GROUP BY p."publication_number"
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 6. Description: Extract the calendar year (first four digits of publication_date) for Canadian patents
SELECT "publication_number",
       FLOOR("publication_date" / 10000) AS "pub_year"
FROM PATENTS.PATENTS.PUBLICATIONS
WHERE "country_code" = 'CA'
  AND "publication_date" BETWEEN 19600101 AND 20201231
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 7. Description: Build the 5‑year bucket start year for every Canadian publication between 1960‑2020
SELECT "publication_number",
       FLOOR("publication_date" / 10000) AS "pub_year",
       1960 + 5 * FLOOR((FLOOR("publication_date" / 10000) - 1960) / 5) AS "five_year_bucket"
FROM PATENTS.PATENTS.PUBLICATIONS
WHERE "country_code" = 'CA'
  AND "publication_date" BETWEEN 19600101 AND 20201231
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 8. Description: Combine inventor counting with 5‑year bucketing; keep only publications that list at least one inventor
WITH inventor_per_patent AS (
    SELECT p."publication_number",
           1960 + 5 * FLOOR((FLOOR(p."publication_date" / 10000) - 1960) / 5) AS "five_year_bucket",
           COUNT(f.value) AS "inventor_cnt"
    FROM PATENTS.PATENTS.PUBLICATIONS p,
         LATERAL FLATTEN(input => p."inventor") f
    WHERE p."country_code" = 'CA'
      AND p."publication_date" BETWEEN 19600101 AND 20201231
    GROUP BY p."publication_number", p."publication_date"
)
SELECT *
FROM inventor_per_patent
WHERE "inventor_cnt" > 0
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 9. Description: For each 5‑year span, calculate (a) total Canadian publications, (b) average inventor count
WITH inventor_per_patent AS (
    SELECT p."publication_number",
           1960 + 5 * FLOOR((FLOOR(p."publication_date" / 10000) - 1960) / 5) AS "five_year_bucket",
           COUNT(f.value) AS "inventor_cnt"
    FROM PATENTS.PATENTS.PUBLICATIONS p,
         LATERAL FLATTEN(input => p."inventor") f
    WHERE p."country_code" = 'CA'
      AND p."publication_date" BETWEEN 19600101 AND 20201231
    GROUP BY p."publication_number", p."publication_date"
)
SELECT "five_year_bucket"             AS "period_start",
       COUNT(*)                       AS "publication_cnt",
       AVG("inventor_cnt")            AS "avg_inventors"
FROM inventor_per_patent
WHERE "inventor_cnt" > 0
GROUP BY "five_year_bucket"
ORDER BY "period_start"
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 10. Description: Same as #9 but expanded to list the ending year of each 5‑year bucket, returning the final answer set
WITH inventor_per_patent AS (
    SELECT p."publication_number",
           1960 + 5 * FLOOR((FLOOR(p."publication_date" / 10000) - 1960) / 5) AS "period_start",
           COUNT(f.value) AS "inventor_cnt"
    FROM PATENTS.PATENTS.PUBLICATIONS p,
         LATERAL FLATTEN(input => p."inventor") f
    WHERE p."country_code" = 'CA'
      AND p."publication_date" BETWEEN 19600101 AND 20201231
    GROUP BY p."publication_number", p."publication_date"
),
aggregated AS (
    SELECT "period_start",
           "period_start" + 4                 AS "period_end",
           COUNT(*)                           AS "publication_cnt",
           AVG("inventor_cnt")                AS "avg_inventors"
    FROM inventor_per_patent
    WHERE "inventor_cnt" > 0
    GROUP BY "period_start"
)
SELECT *
FROM aggregated
ORDER BY "period_start"
LIMIT 20;
```

Each successive query lets you inspect the data, check assumptions, and finally compute the requested averages and publication counts for Canadian patents across 5‑year windows from 1960‑2020.
